(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-most-likely-45"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2: Most Likely 45 (Version 1)","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 24-year-old woman asks how long she can keep using her redness-relief drops. What do you tell her?","choices":["As long as her eyes stay red","No more than two weeks","No more than six weeks","No more than 72 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and if there is no improvement within 72 hours she should stop and be seen. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A toddler swallows a bottle of a parent's redness-relief drops. Why is this dangerous, given the drug acts on alpha-1 receptors in the eye?","choices":["Systemically they act on beta-1 receptors","They are converted to epinephrine in the gut","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase systemically","Systemically they act on alpha-2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local alpha-1 against systemic alpha-2 is why an ingestion behaves differently from an instillation: a small child can develop a slow heart rate, low blood pressure and central nervous system depression. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which ocular glucocorticoids are called soft steroids because they carry a lower risk of raising intraocular pressure?","choices":["Dexamethasone, prednisolone and difluprednate","Triamcinolone, dexamethasone and prednisolone","Fluorometholone, loteprednol and rimexolone","Prednisolone, loteprednol and difluprednate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These three are marked as the soft steroids because they carry a lower risk of raising eye pressure, which matters most in a patient who has glaucoma or is at risk of it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 62-year-old is newly diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma and has risk factors. Which class do you start?","choices":["A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","A topical glucocorticoid","A cholinergic agonist","A prostaglandin analog"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — first line and the most commonly used, dosed once daily. A beta blocker is the other reasonable start. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with asthma needs a beta blocker eye drop for glaucoma. Which agent is the best choice?","choices":["Carteolol","Levobunolol","Betaxolol","Timolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Betaxolol is the beta-1 selective eye drop, so it carries the least risk of airway narrowing; the nonselective agents work better in the eye but add airway resistance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 68-year-old on a prostaglandin analog still has inadequate pressure control. What is the principle for adding a second agent?","choices":["Add a second prostaglandin analog","Increase the prostaglandin to twice daily","Add a topical steroid","Add an agent with a DIFFERENT mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Targeting a second route gives synergy, and fewer drops improves compliance. Which brand pairs which two drugs matters far less than the principle. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic must be avoided outright in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Trimethoprim with polymyxin B","Bacitracin","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, and it works by antagonizing p-aminobenzoic acid to block folic acid synthesis. A documented sulfonamide allergy rules it out — one of the few genuine contraindications in the whole ophthalmic antibiotic list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has bacterial conjunctivitis and keratitis has been ruled out. Which class is preferred?","choices":["A macrolide","A fluoroquinolone","An aminoglycoside","A sulfonamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens use raises the risk of Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod, and the fluoroquinolones are the class that covers it; they are also preferred for corneal ulcers for the same reason. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 33-year-old asks for a prescription for the numbing drops that helped during foreign body removal. What do you do?","choices":["Decline — not for home use","Prescribe a short course for comfort","Prescribe them with a warning not to drive","Substitute a topical steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The eye stays numb for 10 to 20 minutes with no blink reflex, so a patient who keeps treating himself at home can injure the cornea without feeling it; it is unsafe unsupervised. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A patient has used a topical decongestant spray daily for three weeks and is more blocked than when they started. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound congestion, rhinitis medicamentosa","They have developed allergic rhinitis","Bacterial sinusitis has developed","Tolerance, with no rebound effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The drug produces the symptom it was bought to relieve, which is why the patient keeps using it and why the cycle is hard to break. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which otic ingredient is specifically not recommended when the tympanic membrane is perforated or tubes are in place?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Dexamethasone","Polymyxin B","Ofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With a route to the middle ear it risks cochlear damage and hearing loss, which makes an intact drum the thing to establish before prescribing it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 45-year-old long-haul truck driver has seasonal allergic rhinitis and drives 12 hours a day. Which antihistamine is the most appropriate choice for him?","choices":["Diphenhydramine","Fexofenadine","Hydroxyzine","Promethazine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A second-generation histamine-1 (H1) blocker largely stays out of the central nervous system and is rated very low for sedation, so it treats the allergy without making a driver drowsy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slides 32 and 37"},{"q":"Which cardiac adverse effect is associated with ketoconazole?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","QTc prolongation","Heart block","Cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It matters most alongside its CYP3A4 inhibition, because the drugs whose levels it raises may prolong the QT interval too. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A child has a fever during chickenpox. Why must aspirin be avoided?","choices":["It is ineffective against viral fever","It causes irreversible hearing loss in children","It interacts with the varicella vaccine","It raises the risk of Reye's syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A viral illness plus aspirin in a child is the exact combination that produces it, which is why the rule is about the setting rather than the drug alone. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A child with acute otitis media was started on amoxicillin and has not improved after three days. What is the next step?","choices":["Continue the same agent for a further three days","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Move straight to injected ceftriaxone","Stop antibiotics and observe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failure of first-line amoxicillin at three days is the trigger to step up, and amoxicillin-clavulanate is that step; a third-generation cephalosporin is the other step-up option listed. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which statement describes how aspirin inhibits cyclooxygenase in platelets?","choices":["Irreversibly, so the platelet never recovers","Reversibly, so the effect ends as the drug is cleared","Selectively at cyclooxygenase-2 only","Indirectly, by reducing prostaglandin release from storage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A platelet has no nucleus and cannot make fresh enzyme, so one exposure disables it for its lifetime; ibuprofen binds reversibly, which is why its effect fades within about a day. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient on a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor starts taking ibuprofen regularly. What should be watched?","choices":["Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid function","Renal function","Serum potassium only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two interactions at once: ibuprofen with a diuretic puts the kidney at risk, and ibuprofen blunts the ACE inhibitor, so the blood pressure response also needs watching. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What should a patient who drinks alcohol regularly be told about acetaminophen?","choices":["Alcohol prevents acetaminophen from working","The combination causes gastric ulceration","There is no interaction between the two drugs","The combination risks liver damage over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic alcohol use together with acetaminophen increases the risk of liver damage, so a regular drinker is told to cut back or avoid it; an occasional drink is not the concern. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 52-year-old with type 2 diabetes and newly diagnosed hypertension needs a first agent. Which class is preferred for its kidney-protective effect?","choices":["An ACE inhibitor","A beta blocker","An alpha-1 blocker","A direct vasodilator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors are preferred in diabetics because they lower glomerular pressure and slow diabetic nephropathy; an angiotensin receptor blocker is the equivalent choice. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient on enalapril has a bothersome dry cough but well-controlled blood pressure. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to a different ACE inhibitor","Halve the dose of enalapril","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Add a beta blocker to suppress it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not affect bradykinin metabolism, so they do not cause cough, and they keep the benefit of blocking angiotensin II. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A patient taking lisinopril and amlodipine remains above goal after titration, and adherence and measurement have been confirmed. What is the next step?","choices":["Add losartan","Add hydralazine","Add a thiazide-like diuretic","Swap amlodipine for verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker plus a dihydropyridine, the next step is a thiazide-like diuretic; if pressure is still uncontrolled, it is apparent resistant hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with chronic kidney disease who uses a salt substitute is started on lisinopril. Which laboratory abnormality is most important to anticipate?","choices":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is most often seen with renal disease and in those taking potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements or salt substitutes; this patient has two of those. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker would be used to slow the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Diltiazem","Nicardipine","Nimodipine","Isradipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem strongly suppresses the sinoatrial node and atrioventricular conduction; atrial fibrillation or flutter is an approved non-dihydropyridine indication. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A patient wants to stop his metoprolol abruptly before a trip. What is the danger?","choices":["Hypoglycemia, sweating or confusion","Bronchospasm, wheeze or cough","Angina, infarction or a pressure surge","Hyperkalemia, weakness or palpitations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden withdrawal can cause acute angina, myocardial infarction or a marked rise in blood pressure, so the drug is withdrawn slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 72-year-old starting verapamil asks which change in bowel habit to watch for. What should she be told?","choices":["Black stools","Oily, fatty stools","Bloody diarrhea","Constipation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Constipation is among the listed gastrointestinal effects of non-dihydropyridines, so bowel habits are worth asking about at follow-up. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which beta blocker also blocks alpha-1 receptors?","choices":["Metoprolol","Atenolol","Propranolol","Carvedilol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol and labetalol are the third-generation exceptions that add alpha-1 blockade; carvedilol is also antioxidant and antiproliferative. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Hydralazine is N-acetylated in the liver. Which patients are at higher risk of its lupus syndrome?","choices":["Fast acetylators","Slow acetylators","Patients on low doses","Patients on short courses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow acetylators clear hydralazine more slowly; with high doses, long-term use, female sex and Caucasian ancestry, this raises the risk of lupus syndrome. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A patient with a fasting triglyceride of 600 mg/dL needs cholesterol treatment. Which class is contraindicated?","choices":["Fibric acid derivatives","Nicotinic acid (niacin)","Bile acid sequestrants","HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides above 400 mg/dL are an absolute contraindication to resins (above 200 mg/dL is relative), because they can raise VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production and triglycerides. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient's only lipid problem is a triglyceride level over 1000 mg/dL. Which class is most appropriate?","choices":["A fibrate","A bile acid sequestrant","Ezetimibe","A PCSK9 inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL are the primary fibrate indication; fibrates lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% (niacin by a similar amount). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient on verapamil needs a statin. Which statin would CYP3A4 inhibition affect least?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin has minimal CYP metabolism, so verapamil's CYP3A4 inhibition matters least; atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are CYP3A4 substrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient starting niacin asks how to reduce the flushing. What is recommended?","choices":["Take it with alcohol","Take it with grapefruit juice","Take aspirin beforehand","Take it with an antacid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flushing is prostaglandin mediated and is minimized by premedication with aspirin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why is a statin combined with a fibrate only with caution?","choices":["Higher risk of myopathy","Loss of LDL lowering","Higher risk of flushing","Higher risk of hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both classes can cause myopathy, so they are combined cautiously; gemfibrozil is a relative contraindication with statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient taking colestipol also takes thyroxine. How should the thyroxine be timed?","choices":["1 hour before or 4 hours after","At the same time as the resin","30 minutes after the resin","Timing does not matter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resins interfere with absorption of digoxin, warfarin, thyroxine, beta blockers and thiazide diuretics; giving the drug 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin avoids it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which patient falls into one of the four major statin benefit groups?","choices":["A 35-year-old with diabetes and LDL of 120","A 55-year-old after a myocardial infarction","A 50-year-old with LDL of 150 and 3% risk","A 30-year-old with LDL of 160 and no other findings"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease is the first benefit group; the others are LDL (low-density lipoprotein) above 190 mg/dL, diabetes at 40 to 75 with LDL 70 to 189, and high estimated risk. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"In which patient is a statin contraindicated?","choices":["A patient aged 50 with diabetes","A patient who is pregnant","A patient after a heart attack","A patient with LDL of 210 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic disease and pregnancy are the statin contraindications. The course teaches avoiding statins in pregnancy; the US Food and Drug Administration relaxed the formal contraindication in 2021, but statins are still stopped in most pregnancies. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which two classes both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein) substantially?","choices":["Resins and ezetimibe","Statins and resins","Ezetimibe and statins","Fibrates and niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (niacin 15 to 35%, fibrates 10 to 35%). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with exertional angina stopped his beta blocker because of vivid nightmares and fatigue. Heart rate is 74 beats per minute and blood pressure is 134/82 mmHg. He has no asthma, conduction disease or heart failure. Which drug should replace it?","choices":["Carvedilol","Diltiazem","Isosorbide dinitrate","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is the initial therapy when a beta blocker is not tolerated, and he has no contraindication to it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with stable angina uses sublingual nitroglycerin as needed. He asks to start tadalafil for erectile dysfunction. What is the main danger of combining them?","choices":["Bradycardia with heart block","Loss of nitrate effect","Profound hypotension","Bronchospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates raise cyclic guanosine monophosphate and phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors block its breakdown, so vasodilation is exaggerated, risking hypotension, infarction or stroke. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which nitrate formulation is used to relieve an acute attack of angina?","choices":["Isosorbide mononitrate","Isosorbide dinitrate","Nitroglycerin ointment","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting nitroglycerin (tablet or spray under the tongue) relieves acute symptoms and can prevent effort-induced angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with stable angina still has exertional attacks three times a week on metoprolol alone. Heart rate is 66 beats per minute, blood pressure is 140/84 mmHg, and conduction and ventricular function are normal. Which added drug best relieves his symptoms?","choices":["Verapamil","Felodipine","Enalapril","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a beta blocker alone fails, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is added, because it lowers wall tension without slowing conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 34-year-old nonsmoker has chest pain that wakes her at 4 a.m., with ST-segment elevation that resolves. Which antianginal class is best avoided?","choices":["Beta blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Long-acting nitrates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is vasospastic (variant) angina; beta blockers may worsen symptoms, while calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce them. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why do verapamil and diltiazem call for caution with a beta blocker?","choices":["Additive reflex tachycardia","Reduced absorption of the beta blocker","Additive bradycardia and conduction delay","Loss of blood pressure lowering effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both lower heart rate and slow atrioventricular conduction, so combining them with a beta blocker adds to these effects. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with stable exertional angina had a myocardial infarction three years ago. Heart rate is 70 beats per minute and blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg. He has no asthma and no conduction disease. In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which class is best avoided?","choices":["Beta blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The traditional comorbidity table says to avoid calcium channel blockers after an infarction and to prefer a beta blocker. Current practice is less absolute and allows a non-dihydropyridine when a beta blocker cannot be used and ventricular function is normal. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has an ST-elevation myocardial infarction two hours after onset. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg and heart rate is 82 beats per minute. He vomited blood and passed black stools this week, and a gastroscopy found a bleeding ulcer. Which drug class should be avoided?","choices":["A fibrinolytic","A beta blocker","A nitrate","An opioid analgesic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active bleeding and serious recent gastrointestinal hemorrhage are fibrinolytic contraindications, because these drugs break down clots and raise bleeding risk. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man wears a nitroglycerin patch around the clock. After several weeks his exertional angina returns despite the same patch. Which change best restores its effect?","choices":["A second patch at night","Daily aspirin added","Ointment around the clock","A daily nitrate-free interval"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Continuous exposure causes tachyphylaxis, so a patch-free interval, such as twelve hours on and twelve hours off, restores the response. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slides 31 and 32"}]};
